3.Use macros to shorten frequent repeating objects(like std::cin or std::cout in main function)

5. vector <int> a1 (n,0);  --> creating vector of n size(n is user desired input) and 0 will be 
will initialized value of all elements which this vector array will have.

6. for(int i = 0 ; i < n ; i++) {cin >> a1[i];} --> can be used to take input of values of a vector 
array from user

7. `ans += (x > h);` --> answer variable will increase 1 if x is greater than h.

8.  `typedef long long ll;` --> after this ll abhinav ; then abhinav will become a long long 
     int variable and use this typedef for creasing valid alias use this before main function means in 
     global scope to access that keyword alias anywhere in the code.
	   > `typedef` is strictly meant for **data types** like `int`, std::vector<int>, or structures, not 
        for objects or functions.

9. `road_w += 2;` --> this will increase road_w variable by 2.

10. `ans += (x>h);` --> increase answer variable by 1 is x is greater than h.

11. #define MACRO_NAME object_or_expression
	  - example - #define out std::cout 
	  - then whenever i will type out in my main function this will do work of std::cout and make sure 
      to define macros in global scope and don't use semicolon with them because they are not statements in c++ 
12. Header files are included using `#include`, which is a **preprocessor directive**.

13. In everyday terms, ==**"decimal representation"**== just means **how a number is written down using regular 
digits (0 through 9)**.
  
   - When you read a programming problem or a math definition that says _"numbers whose decimal representations..."
    _, it simply means **the actual written digits of the number**.

14. string s_rev = t;
      - reverse(s_rev.begin() , s_rev.end()); --> this reverse function is a void function so it directly changes 
        the main string to reverse order and store back to same variable.    
      -  Think of them as a **range boundary**
       -  **`.begin()`**: Points _at_ the **first element**.
       - **`.end()`**: Points _just past_ the **last element** (acting as a stop sign).
       - Together, they form a **`[start, finish)`** window used by algorithms to know where to begin and where 
          to stop.
